IMPORT prelude

-- Fraud Act 2006 (2006 c. 35): THE NOUNS.
--
-- This module holds the entities and the choices that the other modules talk about, and
-- nothing else: no rules and no tests.  Each is a fact that a witness of ordinary competence
-- could testify to.  Whether those facts make an offence out is for the module of the
-- section concerned.
--
-- Source: the plain-text rendering ukpga-2006-35.txt (version 2023-02-07), not the official
-- text.  Every noun says in a comment where in the Act it comes from.
--
-- Naming: field names start at the complement and leave the verb to the genitive, so
-- `case's `acts dishonestly in making it`` reads as the Act reads.  Words in a field name
-- are the Act's own words, with a plain gloss in the comment above.

§ `The nouns of the Fraud Act 2006`

§§ `Gain and loss (section 5)`

-- Plain gloss: the thing a person aims to gain or to cause someone else to lose.
-- Section 5(2): "gain" and "loss" extend only to gain or loss "in money or other property";
-- "property" means any property whether real or personal, including things in action and
-- other intangible property.  A service, information or a favour is none of these.
DECLARE `Thing gained or lost` IS ONE OF
    `money`
    `real property`
    `personal property`
    `a thing in action`
    `other intangible property`
    `something that is neither money nor property`

-- Plain gloss: section 5(3) says a gain includes keeping what one has as well as getting what
-- one does not have.
DECLARE `Way of gaining` IS ONE OF
    `by getting what one does not have`
    `by keeping what one has`

-- Plain gloss: section 5(4) says a loss includes not getting what one might get as well as
-- parting with what one has.
DECLARE `Way of losing` IS ONE OF
    `by parting with what one has`
    `by not getting what one might get`

-- Plain gloss: section 5(2)(b) says gain and loss include a temporary one and a permanent one.
DECLARE `Permanence` IS ONE OF
    `temporary`
    `permanent`

-- A gain the person aims at.
DECLARE `Intended gain` HAS
    `what the gain is in`  IS A `Thing gained or lost`
    `how it is gained`     IS A `Way of gaining`
    `how long it lasts`    IS A `Permanence`

-- A loss the person aims at, or a loss the person aims to expose another to a risk of.
DECLARE `Intended loss` HAS
    `what the loss is in`  IS A `Thing gained or lost`
    `how it is lost`       IS A `Way of losing`
    `how long it lasts`    IS A `Permanence`

-- What the person intends "by making the representation" (section 2(1)(b)), "by failing to
-- disclose the information" (section 3(b)) or "by means of the abuse of that position"
-- (section 4(1)(c)).  NOTHING means that the person does not intend that particular thing.
DECLARE `Aim` HAS
    `a gain for himself or another`                     IS A MAYBE `Intended gain`
    `loss to another`                                   IS A MAYBE `Intended loss`
    `exposing another to a risk of loss`                IS A MAYBE `Intended loss`

§§ `Fraud by false representation (section 2)`

-- Plain gloss: section 2(3) says a representation is one "as to fact or law, including a
-- representation as to the state of mind of the person making the representation or any
-- other person".
DECLARE `What a representation is about` IS ONE OF
    `a fact`
    `the law`
    `the state of mind of the person making it`
    `the state of mind of any other person`
    `something other than fact, law or state of mind`

-- Plain gloss: section 2(4) says a representation may be express or implied.
DECLARE `Form of a representation` IS ONE OF
    `express`
    `implied`

-- Plain gloss: section 2(5) says a representation may be regarded as made if it is submitted
-- in any form to any system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to
-- communications (with or without human intervention).
DECLARE `Who or what a representation was made to` IS ONE OF
    `a person`
    `a system or device designed to receive, convey or respond to communications`
    `nobody and nothing: it was not communicated`

DECLARE `Representation` HAS
    `what it is about`                                                           IS A `What a representation is about`
    `its form`                                                                   IS A `Form of a representation`
    `who or what it was made to`                                                 IS A `Who or what a representation was made to`
    `untrue`                                                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `misleading`                                                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person making it knows that it is, or might be, untrue or misleading`   IS A BOOLEAN

-- One alleged act of fraud by false representation.
DECLARE `False representation case` HAS
    `the representation`                           IS A `Representation`
    `acts dishonestly in making it`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the person intends by making it`         IS AN `Aim`

§§ `Fraud by failing to disclose information (section 3)`

-- One alleged act of fraud by failing to disclose information.
DECLARE `Non-disclosure case` HAS
    `fails to disclose information to another person`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the information is information which he is under a legal duty to disclose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `acts dishonestly in failing to disclose it`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the person intends by failing to disclose it`                         IS AN `Aim`

§§ `Fraud by abuse of position (section 4)`

-- Plain gloss: section 4(2) says a person may be regarded as having abused a position even
-- though the conduct consisted of an omission rather than an act.
DECLARE `Form of conduct` IS ONE OF
    `an act`
    `an omission`

-- One alleged act of fraud by abuse of position.
DECLARE `Abuse of position case` HAS
    `occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard, or not to act against, the financial interests of another person`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `abuses that position`                                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the conduct that abuses it was`                                            IS A `Form of conduct`
    `acts dishonestly in abusing it`                                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `what the person intends by means of the abuse`                             IS AN `Aim`

§§ `Articles for use in frauds (sections 6 to 8)`

-- Plain gloss: section 8(1) says "article" includes any program or data held in electronic
-- form, for sections 6 and 7.  The ordinary sense of "article" is not defined by the Act.
DECLARE `Article case` HAS
    `an article in the ordinary sense of the word`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `a program held in electronic form`              IS A BOOLEAN
    `data held in electronic form`                   IS A BOOLEAN

-- Section 6(1): possession or control of an article.
DECLARE `Possession case` HAS
    `the article`                                                                IS AN `Article case`
    `in his possession`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `under his control`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `for use in the course of or in connection with any fraud`                   IS A BOOLEAN

-- Plain gloss: section 7(1) lists four things a person may do with an article.
DECLARE `Dealing with an article` IS ONE OF
    `makes it`
    `adapts it`
    `supplies it`
    `offers to supply it`
    `does none of these`

-- Section 7(1): making, adapting, supplying or offering to supply an article.
DECLARE `Making or supplying case` HAS
    `the article`                                                                IS AN `Article case`
    `what the person does with it`                                               IS A `Dealing with an article`
    `knowing that it is designed or adapted for use in the course of or in connection with fraud`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `intending it to be used to commit, or assist in the commission of, fraud`   IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Participating in fraudulent business (section 9)`

-- Section 9: a business carried on by a person outside the reach of section 993 of the
-- Companies Act 2006 (fraudulent trading).  The Companies Act is not among the sources, so the
-- facts about who carries on the business are asked as facts.
DECLARE `Fraudulent business case` HAS
    `knowingly a party to the carrying on of the business`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `the business is carried on by a company (as defined in section 1(1) of the Companies Act 2006)`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the business is carried on by a person to whom section 993 applies as if the person were a company`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the business is carried on by a person exempted from the application of section 993`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the business is carried on with intent to defraud creditors of any person`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the business is carried on for any other fraudulent purpose (as in section 993)`   IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Obtaining services dishonestly (section 11)`

-- Plain gloss: section 11(2)(b) turns on whether payment was made, and made in full.
DECLARE `Payment for services` IS ONE OF
    `no payment was made`
    `payment was made, but not in full`
    `payment was made in full`

DECLARE `Obtaining services case` HAS
    `obtains services for himself or another`                                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `does so by a dishonest act`                                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the services are made available on the basis that payment has been, is being or will be made for or in respect of them`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `payment for the services`                                                    IS A `Payment for services`
    `when he obtains them, he knows that they are being made available on that basis`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `when he obtains them, he knows that they might be made available on that basis`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `he intends that payment will not be made, or will not be made in full`       IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Liability of company officers (section 12)`

-- Plain gloss: the people section 12 reaches.  The last-but-one is section 12(3).
DECLARE `Capacity in a body corporate` IS ONE OF
    `a director`
    `a manager`
    `a secretary`
    `another similar officer`
    `a person purporting to act in any such capacity`
    `a member of a body corporate whose affairs are managed by its members`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Officer case` HAS
    `an offence under this Act is committed by a body corporate`                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is proved that the offence was committed with the person's consent`       IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is proved that the offence was committed with the person's connivance`    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person's capacity`                                                       IS A `Capacity in a body corporate`
    `the acts and defaults are in connection with his functions of management`    IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Evidence (section 13)`

-- Section 13(3): the proceedings in which section 13(1) bites.
DECLARE `Kind of proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `proceedings for the recovery or administration of any property`
    `proceedings for the execution of a trust`
    `proceedings for an account of any property or dealings with property`
    `other proceedings`

-- Section 13(1): what the person is asked or ordered to do.
DECLARE `What is required of the person` IS ONE OF
    `to answer a question put to him`
    `to comply with an order made`
    `neither`

-- Section 13(1): who the answer or compliance may incriminate.
DECLARE `Who may be incriminated` IS ONE OF
    `the person`
    `the person's spouse or civil partner`
    `neither of them`

-- Section 13(1), (2), (4): the offence concerned.  "Related offence" is conspiracy to defraud
-- or any other offence involving any form of fraudulent conduct or purpose.
DECLARE `Kind of offence` IS ONE OF
    `an offence under this Act`
    `conspiracy to defraud`
    `any other offence involving any form of fraudulent conduct or purpose`
    `an offence of none of these kinds`

-- Section 13(1): a question put, or an order made, in proceedings.
DECLARE `Question or order case` HAS
    `the proceedings are`                                      IS A `Kind of proceedings`
    `what is required of the person`                           IS A `What is required of the person`
    `the ground is that doing so may incriminate`              IS A `Who may be incriminated`
    `of an offence of the kind`                                IS A `Kind of offence`

-- Section 13(2): against whom the statement is to be used.
DECLARE `Against whom a statement is to be used` IS ONE OF
    `the person who made it`
    `the spouse or civil partner of the person who made it`
    `anyone else`

-- Section 13(2): a statement or admission made in answering such a question or complying with
-- such an order.
DECLARE `Statement case` HAS
    `made in answering such a question or complying with such an order`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the proceedings in which it is to be used are for an offence of the kind`  IS A `Kind of offence`
    `it is to be used against`                                                  IS AN `Against whom a statement is to be used`
    `they married or became civil partners after the making of the statement or admission`   IS A BOOLEAN

-- The two answers section 13 can give.  The second names the case where section 13 says
-- nothing, so that the Act's silence is not mistaken for permission or for refusal.
DECLARE `Answer under section 13(1)` IS ONE OF
    `not excused on that ground`
    `section 13(1) does not apply`

DECLARE `Answer under section 13(2)` IS ONE OF
    `not admissible in evidence`
    `section 13(2) does not apply`

§§ `Penalties (sections 1, 6, 7, 9 and 11)`

-- The offences this Act creates, and two names the Act uses that carry no penalty here.
DECLARE `Offence in or named by this Act` IS ONE OF
    `fraud (section 1)`
    `possession etc. of articles for use in frauds (section 6)`
    `making or supplying articles for use in frauds (section 7)`
    `participating in fraudulent business carried on by sole trader etc. (section 9)`
    `obtaining services dishonestly (section 11)`
    `participating in fraudulent business carried on by company etc.: penalty (section 10, blank in this text)`
    `conspiracy to defraud (named in section 13(4); this Act creates no such offence)`

DECLARE `Place` IS ONE OF
    `England`
    `Wales`
    `Northern Ireland`
    `Scotland`
    `anywhere else`

-- Plain gloss: the longest term of imprisonment on summary conviction that the Act states.
-- The Act gives a figure only for Northern Ireland (section 1(4) and its twins) and, through
-- paragraph 1 of Schedule 2, for an offence committed before 2 May 2022.  Elsewhere it says
-- "the general limit in a magistrates' court" and states no figure.
DECLARE `Summary imprisonment limit` IS ONE OF
    `six months`
    `the general limit in a magistrates' court (the Act states no figure)`

-- Plain gloss: "the statutory maximum" is a defined amount in other law; the Act states no
-- figure.  For a fine on indictment the Act says only "a fine", with no limit.
DECLARE `Fine limit` IS ONE OF
    `the statutory maximum (the Act states no figure)`
    `no limit is stated`

DECLARE `Mode of trial` IS ONE OF
    `summary conviction`
    `conviction on indictment`

-- What the Act says may follow a conviction.
DECLARE `Penalty` HAS
    `on summary conviction, imprisonment for a term not exceeding`       IS A `Summary imprisonment limit`
    `on summary conviction, a fine not exceeding`                         IS A `Fine limit`
    `on conviction on indictment, imprisonment for a term not exceeding, in years`   IS A NUMBER
    `on conviction on indictment, a fine not exceeding`                   IS A `Fine limit`
    `imprisonment and a fine may both be imposed`                         IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `Schedule 2 (transitional provisions and savings)`

-- Schedule 2 paragraph 3: what becomes of liability for one of the old deception offences.
DECLARE `Outcome for an old deception offence` IS ONE OF
    `liability is saved by paragraph 3 of Schedule 2`
    `the offence was abolished before any of its relevant events: no liability under it`
    `the Act does not say (every relevant event was before commencement, or none is given)`
